=TITLE CHANGES 

=head1 Upcoming

=head1 0.0.94


=head2 podlite

=item C<lint> warns about C<:notify> on a C<=nested> block, written on the block or given by C<=set> or C<=config>; the specification writes the notification attribute C<:notice>, and a block with C<:notify> and without C<:notice> is drawn as a plain nested block (rule C<notify-attribute>)

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item the list of attribute names holds C<notice>, the name of the notification attribute, in place of C<notify>

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item B<Breaking:> a notification block is drawn by C<:notice>, the name the specification gives the attribute. A C<=nested> block with C<:notify> and without C<:notice> is drawn as a plain nested block, without a title and without folding. The classes of the output keep their names C<notify> and C<notify-title>

=head1 0.0.93


=head2 @podlite/schema

=item the virtual margin of a block ends with the block: text indented under an ended heading or paragraph block is read against the margin of the block around it, and is code when set in from that margin; it took the margin of the ended block and was a paragraph

=item text at the top level of a document whose first line is set in from the margin is code, as inside C<=pod>; it was a paragraph. A line of spaces alone stays as it was

=item the selector C<ambient> finds text outside any block in the default mode, and C<*> finds it with the blocks; it found nothing. The C<Ambient> node carries an optional C<config>, as C<Para> and C<Code> do

=head1 0.0.92


=head2 podlite

=item C<lint> warns about a block named C<=Include>, C<=Set>, C<=Config> or C<=Alias>, which is a named block and not the directive (rule C<directive-named-block>), and about C<=Image>, which the specification writes as C<=picture> (rule C<image-named-block>); each rule can be turned off on its own

=item C<assembleIncludes> takes C<reportCycles>: an include whose path names a source already on the way in is then reported as C<cycle> even when it loses no C<=set>; a mask that finds the file it is written in still passes it by without a word

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item B<Breaking:> the include plugin assembles C<=include> with the assembler of C<podlite>. The blocks an include finds take the place of the directive, and no C<include> node is left around them. They are read from the text of their source, with the settings in effect where the directive stands, and the includes inside them are assembled as well. The plugin takes C<catalogue>, the records the site read before the plugins ran: an include finds its source there, as well as among the documents plugins make later in the chain. Without it the plugin says so and looks among the records at hand. A relative path in a C<file:> source is resolved from the file the directive is written in; before, the source was the record whose path ended with the written one. A C<doc:> name that two documents answer to resolves to neither, and the refusal names both. With C<bounds>, a path written in one of those directories does not lead out of it

=item a C<=set> before an include that brings no block reaches the next block

=item B<Breaking:> an include that does not place what it names stops the build with C<IncludeError>, one line for each such include, with the file and the line of the directive and the files it came through. It stops on a missing source or address; a selector that cannot be read; a scheme the site does not read; a name that answers to two documents; a file that includes itself. It was a warning, and the page was published without the included text

=item C<includePasses> gives the include plugin in two passes over one catalogue. C<first> goes before the plugins of images, links and React and places what it can; an include of a document a plugin makes later in the chain, the site data or a name given in C<late>, waits there. C<last> goes after them and places only such documents; one that holds an include, or brings a picture or a link, stops the build. The site data plugin exports the name of its document as C<SITE_DATA_DOCUMENT>

=item a record is read with the body of C<=React> as Podlite in its place, by the reader an include reads the texts it brings with: an include inside the body is assembled, and the React plugin keeps the blocks of the body as the content of the block rather than reading the body again into a nested list

=item the tables of contents written in a record are built again once the includes are in, and take in the headings of a C<=React> body; a table an include brought stays as its own file built it

=item the header, the footer and the description of a record are assembled as well, each from the file it was written in. A template is assembled as a record of the chain; a template a record carries is left as it is

=item the images, links and React plugins find a path written in a block an include brought from the file the block was written in, not from the file of the record: a picture, a C<file:> link and a component import of an included chapter work as they do in the chapter itself

=item a link to a file placed at more than one address goes to the address a search engine is told to index: the current version of a document, not its permanent address marked C<:noindex>. The map of addresses is keyed by the absolute path of a file

=item B<Breaking:> a C<doc:> link whose name no published document answers to, or that two documents answer to, stops the build with C<LinkError>, naming the file and the line. So does a link to a section the document does not have; a link to a section goes to the address the page gives that section. The link was replaced by the words "not found", and of two documents the last one won. The links plugin takes C<documents>, the files the include plugin finds a name in (C<includePasses> gives it), so that a link and an include read a name alike, and C<home>, the file of the home page, whose address C</> the site data plugin gives only later. A link to the section a document is named by goes to the page itself. C<doc:#section> goes to a section of the page the link is on. A name an include brought into a document is not a name of that document

=item C<nodeOrigin> gives the file a node of a record was written in, when an include brought it there. The mark is held under a symbol key on the node, so a copy made by spreading the node keeps it and JSON does not carry it

=item C<=Include> is no longer read as C<=include>: by the specification it is a named block. The lint of C<podlite> warns about it

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item the card of a test stays inside the window: a line of a test at the right edge of the window opened its card past the edge

=item C<revealTest(id)> opens the test with that C<:id> on the page, with the folded sections above it, and brings its line into view. A click on a link to the address the page already shows fires no C<hashchange>, so a site calls it on such a click

=head1 0.0.91


=head2 podlite

=item a C<=set> before C<=include> reaches a paragraph or code written without a marker that the include brings first, and an include failing after such a paragraph no longer reports the assignment lost

=item C<podlite test> runs a test under each configuration that reaches a paragraph or code written without a marker in it

=item a file is read in the format its name gives: C<.podlite> and C<.pod6> as Podlite, C<.md> and C<.markdown> as Markdown, any other extension or none in the default mode, where text outside blocks is ambient; the extension is compared without regard to case. This holds for an included file, for the document C<convert>, C<query> and C<podlite test> are given, and for C<lint>; text from the standard input stays Podlite. A C<.md> file brought by C<=include> was read as Podlite, and a C<.txt> file as Podlite paragraphs

=item a C<:mime-type> written on the C<=include> line after the address of the source, before a selection, decides how that file is read: C<text/podlite>, C<text/markdown>, and any other C<text/> type in the default mode; a type that is not text is an error of the new kind C<format>, and the include brings nothing. A configuration that does not read whole leaves the line unread. It was taken as part of the file name

=item an included Markdown file gets the C<=config> of the including file on its blocks

=item C<formatOfFile>, C<formatOfType> and C<writtenTree> are exported; C<ReadFormat> has the value C<default>. A source may carry C<format>; the fourth argument of the reader and of C<parse> in an include assembly may carry C<format> for that one reading

=item C<podlite convert> and C<podlite query> warn when an included file comes from outside the root: the directory of each input file, or the current directory for stdin. C<--root> sets one root for every input. A file is outside by its real path, with links followed. The warning names the directive and the path as written, gives a mask the number of its files that are outside, and is given only when blocks of such a file stand in the assembled document; C<query> gives it only for blocks it prints

=item C<podlite convert> prints the file and line of the directive in front of every include warning

=item a source may carry C<external>, the reason it is external; an include whose blocks from such sources stand in the assembled document is reported once as an C<external> warning, with those sources in C<sources>; a comment counts, as it stands in the document. C<externalMessage> gives the text of such a warning for the sources a host shows. The origin of a node lists the directives it came through in C<steps>. C<resolveIncludes> takes C<root>, and a C<SourceProvider> may give C<real>, the path of a file with links followed

=item a mask of C<sourcesFromFiles> is resolved like a path, its C<.> and C<..> steps taken, and stays in its directory: C<*.podlite> no longer names files in directories below, as on disk; C<**> goes down

=item C<sourcesFromFiles> is a root of its own: a path with or without a leading slash names the same text, so a host can give its files by plain relative paths; a source keeps the identity it had

=item the lint context takes C<sources>, C<context> and C<self>: the C<include-resolves> rule checks a text that is not read from disk against the sources a host gives

=item C<podlite/lib/refresh-tocs> is exported

=item C<readerFor> takes C<body>: a block the host says yes to has its raw body read as Podlite in its place, with the settings in effect where the block stands; the blocks of the body stand at their places in the text, and an include assembly given the same reader brings what an C<=include> written in the body names

=item a provider of include sources names the address schemes it resolves in C<schemes>, C<file> alone when it names none, and C<locate> is given the scheme as its last argument: C<=include doc:Name> and an operand C<in doc:Name> reach a provider that lists C<doc>. A path that is not a mask names one source. When a provider says in C<failed> why it cannot give one, the problem carries the address and that reason and the directive stays in the tree; two sources with different ids for such a path are refused the same way. A source that is not found, with no reason given, is reported as before. A document that includes itself is told from its id before it is read. C<SourceStore.located> takes the scheme as its last argument

=item C<podlite lint> reads an included Podlite file with the Markdown and table of contents plugins and with the settings in effect at the directive: a heading written in a Markdown section of an included file is found by its address; it was reported as not found. A warning a plugin prints while an included file is read stays off the console, and an included file that cannot be read costs one finding and leaves the others in place

=item C<readerFor> makes the reading an include assembly asks for out of a parser, so a host need not write it; its fourth argument hands the parser the lists it fills with recognition events and diagnostics

=item the assembly of includes is exported from C<podlite> as well as from C<podlite/assemble>. C<locate> may say why a mask could not be expanded, in C<failed>; with C<tolerant> a text that fails to parse is a source that cannot be had

=item a table of contents written in an included file lists what that file includes: it is built again over the file once its own includes are in, also when the table alone is brought in by a selector. It listed the file as it was before them

=item C<podlite/assemble> gives C<sourcesFromFiles>, sources over texts held in memory by path, and C<assembleAsync>, which assembles a document whose sources answer through promises: it asks for what is missing and assembles again until nothing is. C<createSourceStore> keeps the answers between assemblies. Sources may answer C<undefined> for what is not known yet; the include then stays in place with its C<=set> assignments and nothing is reported

=item the assembly of includes is given as C<podlite/assemble>, an entry with no module of Node in it: C<assembleIncludes> takes the included text from C<sources> the host gives, where C<locate> finds the sources a written path names and C<read> gives the text of one. C<resolveIncludes> reads the disk through it and is not changed. An include problem that loses C<=set> assignments names them in C<lost>

=item a file an C<=include> brings whole stays in a C<root> block in the assembled document, so its C<=config> and C<=alias> end with it: in HTML and Markdown they acted on the blocks after the include. A selection goes through the block and does not find it

=item C<podlite test> runs a test brought in by several files once for each configuration it is brought in under; it ran it once whatever the configuration

=item on the first block an C<=include> brings, a C<=set> of the included file wins over a C<=set> written before the include for the same key; the one before the include won

=item a C<=config> written before C<=include> reaches the blocks the include places, as it reaches the blocks written after it; an included file kept to its own C<=config>. The nearer file decides an option: the file of the block, then the files including it, nearest first. The included text is read with those settings, so a C<:allow> they give opens its blocks to markup codes. C<convert>, C<query> and C<test> follow the rule

=item the selector of an C<=include> still reads the file without the settings of the including file. A found block those settings read as something else is placed as its file reads on its own, with the warning C<include-reading-differs>

=item C<resolveIncludes> hands C<parse> the settings in effect at the directive as a third argument

=item a C<=set> written before C<=include> reaches the first block the include brings, in C<convert>, C<query> and C<podlite test>; the block after the include received it. When the include brings no block, the assignment goes on to the next block of the same list, and to the end of the list without one it is reported with the kind C<set-target>. When the include fails, it goes nowhere, and the message of the failure names it; an include that brings nothing because its file is already being included says so with the kind C<cycle>, and does so only when it loses an assignment. Both kinds are warnings, and C<isWarning> is exported. An include that fails inside the included content, before any block, stops the search the same way: the assignment goes nowhere and its message names it. C<podlite lint> reads the document without its includes, so a link to an address a C<=set> gives an included block is reported as not found; before, the address landed on the block after the include

=item C<podlite query> reads a file its selector names, relative to the working directory, as it reads a file an operand of C<in> names: C<podlite query 'file:x.podlite | para'> needs no input files, a mask is expanded on disk as C<=include> expands it, and the files and input given are not read, with a warning. A source that does not resolve, and an address no file holds, is reported once and exits with 1; it used to give 0 matches. A file given is no longer taken for a source that only shares its tail, and a C<doc:> source is looked for among the files given

=item an include by a selector places a found block once: a block inside another found block comes with it and is not placed again, so C<=include part.podlite | *> brings a heading inside a C<=pod> once, and one table of contents. With C<*> the include brings no C<=alias> written at the top of the file, and an C<A<>> code that relies on it is left as written; an include without a selector still brings the whole file

=item C<podlite query> selects from the tree C<convert> and C<podlite test> read: a Markdown section is read into blocks, a table from C<data:> into rows and cells, and tables of contents are made again after the includes. A block with no place of its own in the file is given in C<--to podlite> as its Markdown, a row of such a table as one line of cells; in C<--to json> a block of a Markdown section carries the place of the section and C<precision: section>. The root inside a section and a folded section are not counted as found. C<--to md> and C<--to html> hide what the document hides. A query loads the Markdown, image and table of contents plugins when it runs, not the diagram renderer. The rules are shared through the entry C<podlite/lib/query-blocks>

=item tables of contents are made again after the includes inside the block that holds them, and C<podlite test> finds a C<=toc> block

=item C<podlite query>, C<=include> and C<podlite test> read a C<file:> operand of C<in> relative to where the selector is written: the working directory, the file of the directive, the file of the test. An operand that does not resolve is reported: the query exits with 1, the include brings nothing in and C<podlite lint> reports it as an error, and the assertion does not hold

=item podlite test runs the tests written in Podlite files: each C<=test> is reported as passed, failed or skipped, with the assertion that did not hold, the document it read and where a found block is written. C<--against> examines a document with the tests, C<--format json> writes the report as one JSON document, and C<--allow-skipped> lets skipped tests alone pass the run. The exit code is 0 when every test passed, 1 when a test failed or was skipped, no test was found or an include lost tests, and 2 on wrong arguments or a failure of the implementation

=item podlite/test gives the runner to programs: C<runTests> returns the report as data, and C<collectTests>, C<planRuns>, C<inputsFor> and C<evaluateAssertion> are its parts

=item an include whose selector cannot be read, or whose scheme is not C<file:>, is now a warning in podlite convert, podlite query and the include-resolves rule of podlite lint; it used to be left in place without a word

=item podlite --version prints the version of the package

=item podlite convert keeps a table of contents hidden when it is rebuilt after the includes are in

=item podlite convert builds a table of contents of the document after its includes are in, so the table lists the blocks they bring

=item C<:lint-ignore> on a block no longer silences a finding at the first line of the block after it

=item podlite query selects through C<=include>: blocks an include brings in are found, their text and the file in the json output are those of the file they are written in, and a selection of C<include> no longer finds a directive that resolved. An include that is not there is reported with the file and line of the directive, the other matches are still written, and the exit code is 1; the report is not silenced by --quiet

=item podlite convert stops on an include whose address is not in the file, as it already did on a missing file, and on a source that is a directory. The address is found the way a link finds its target: by C<:id>, or by a heading under its name or the form an output gives it

=item when two blocks of an included file share the address, the first is taken and podlite convert warns; the last one used to be taken

=item podlite lint adds the include-resolves rule: an include whose source or address is not there is an error at the directive, a problem inside an included file is reported at the directive that brought it in, and an address that names two blocks is a warning. A selection that finds nothing is not reported. The rule runs on files read from disk only

=head2 @podlite/diagram

=item a hidden diagram shows its source masked and is not drawn, so the drawing cannot show what it hides; its caption is masked too

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item C<includeSource> reads included files from a source that answers through promises: the preview shows the document at once and the included content when it has come, with no warning while a file is awaited. C<invalidateIncludes> on the editor ref forgets what was read for a path, or for everything, and asks again

=head2 @podlite/formula

=item a hidden formula shows its source masked and is not typeset; its caption is masked too

=head2 @podlite/highlight

=item hidden text in a code block reaches the highlighter masked, so it stays hidden once the block is highlighted, and a highlight made for draft is dropped when the page switches to production; the caption of a hidden block is masked too

=head2 @podlite/image

=item the alternative text of a hidden picture is masked

=head2 @podlite/mcp

=item a file of C<files> is read in the format its name gives, any other extension than Podlite or Markdown in the default mode, and a C<:mime-type> on the C<=include> line decides how it is read

=item C<podlite_query> reads a source its selector names (C<file:part.podlite | head1>, a mask, or C<input.podlite> for the document itself) and a C<file:> operand from C<files>; each file found is read with its own includes, and the selection runs over each of them, as C<podlite query> does over files on disk. When the files the selection names do not include the document itself, the answer says that C<text> was not used as its document. Under a mask with an address a file without the address is passed over

=item the json answer of C<podlite_query> gives each block the file it comes from, first

=item C<files> and the document are one set: a file of the set that includes C<input.podlite> gets the document itself

=item a mask of an include in C<files> stays in its directory: C<=include file:*.podlite> no longer brings files of directories below, in C<podlite_render>, C<podlite_query> and C<podlite_validate> alike; C<**> goes down

=item C<podlite_render>, C<podlite_query> and C<podlite_validate> take C<files>, the texts a document includes, by path relative to the document; an C<=include> is assembled from them as C<convert> assembles it from disk. In C<podlite_render> and C<podlite_query> a problem the assembly reports as an error makes the answer an error, with the problems first; other problems and the list of the files whose blocks are in the document come after the output. C<podlite_validate> answers with its report alone The document stands in the map as C<input.podlite>, and a key of that name is refused

=item a call without C<files> on a document with an C<=include> now answers with a second part naming the paths the includes ask for; the output is as before. C<podlite_validate> gives the same as an C<info> problem, and the report stays C<ok>

=item C<podlite_query> reports a source or an address in the selector that does not resolve as an error of the tool; it returned no matches

=item the query tool selects from the tree C<convert> reads, so a Markdown section is read into blocks, and gives a found block as C<podlite query> does.

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item a record keeps the text it was read from: C<recordSource> returns the text, the file name and the type given for it, C<recordOrigin> returns the same for a record the pubdate plugin cut out of a file, and C<isParsedTree> tells whether the tree is still the one that was parsed. C<parseText> parses a given text as a file of that name is read. The source is held under a symbol key, so it is absent from JSON and from C<Object.keys>, and present when a record is printed or compared deeply

=item on the first block an C<=include> brings, a C<=set> of the included file wins over a C<=set> written before the include for the same key; the one before the include won

=item a C<=set> written before C<=include> reaches the first block the include places, and the record the block comes from is left unchanged; the block after the include received it. When the include fails, the warning names the assignment it lost. When it places no block, or its first block is an include inside what was placed, a warning says the assignment has no target. An include whose selector cannot be read, or whose scheme is neither C<doc:> nor C<file:>, says so; it was reported as not found

=item C<=include> whose source or address does not resolve is left in place with one warning; it gave two. An address is found by the text of a heading as well as by C<:id>

=item C<=include> places a found block once: a block inside another found block comes with it and is not placed again

=item an include whose selector has an operand of C<in> that does not resolve is left in place with a warning, rather than stopping the run

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item code written without a marker inside an item, a definition or a C<=nested> block is verbatim unless its own C<:allow> names markup codes, as at the top of a document; it read every markup code, and the C<:allow> of the block around it. A paragraph written without a marker there reads markup codes as an explicit C<=para> in its place does; it read the C<:allow> of the block around it. The text of a cell keeps the codes its table, row and cell allow

=item C<=config para> and C<=config code> reach a paragraph and code written without a marker, in a document, a C<=pod>, an item in every form, a definition, a C<=nested>, a cell, a semantic block and a C<=markdown> block; the text of an explicit C<=para> and of a heading and the term of a definition take none. C<=set> gives its assignments to such a paragraph or code as to the next block. The selection, C<:masked>, C<:allow> and C<:nested> treat them as an explicit C<=para> and C<=code>. Only the explicit block received them

=item the C<Para> and C<Code> nodes carry an optional C<config>, present only when C<=config> or C<=set> gave them settings

=item C<blockNameOf>, C<holderInside> and C<isSetTarget> are exported: which node of a tree is a block and of what name, and where C<=set> assignments stop

=item C<walkConfigScopes> calls a visitor for each block of a tree with the C<=config> settings in effect where the block stands

=item a blank line in the body of an indented C<=begin> block with verbatim content is kept when it is indented less than the block, an empty line among them: an indented C<=begin code> or C<=begin markdown> lost such lines, and paragraphs of Markdown ran together

=item C<runSelector> takes C<readSource>: the host reads the documents an operand with a C<file:> or a C<doc:> source names

=item the body of a C<=markdown> block is read as Markdown whatever C<:allow> it is given, on the block, by a C<=set> before it, by C<=config> or by the settings of an including file; with a code named in C<:allow> the body was read for Podlite markup codes and its headings, lists and paragraphs were lost

=item C<toHtml> writes the blocks of a C<root> block with no warning; it printed the whole document to the error stream as a node left unhandled

=item a C<=config> for C<data-table> reaches the table before it is read: a C<:mime-type> it gives reads an inline body, and an C<:allow> it gives acts in the cells; the table came out empty

=item a C<:mime-type> written on a C<=data-table> with C<:src<data:...>> is the format the data is read in; it was an error. One a C<=config> gives does not replace the format of the data

=item on the first block an C<=include> brings, a C<=set> of the included file wins over a C<=set> written before the include for the same key; the one before the include won

=item a C<=config> or C<=alias> written under a folded heading stays in effect after the section the heading folds; it ended with the section

=item a C<=config> adds its options to an earlier C<=config> for the same block or markup code, and decides the ones both set; it replaced the earlier one whole. HTML and Markdown output follow the same rule

=item C«=config C<> :allow» acts from the line it is written on; it acted on the whole block it was written in

=item C<parse> and C<toAst> take C<config>, the settings in effect where the text is placed; a C<=config> of the text is nearer than they are. C<mergeConfigSettings> joins two lists of settings by that rule

=item a C<toc> node keeps the selector it was built by in C<selector>, unless the selector line holds a markup code; C<mkToc> takes it as its last argument

=item a C<=set> written before C<=include> is kept on the include, in its C<set>, for the first block its content brings; the block after the include no longer receives it. A value a block got from C<=set> is marked C<from: 'set'> in its configuration, a default from C<=config> C<from: 'config'>, and a written one carries no mark. C<applySetToFirst> gives such assignments to the first block of a list, C<mergeSet> joins two lists of them, and C<isSetTransparent> tells a node the search passes

=item C<runSelector> throws C<SelectorError> when the source of a selector does not resolve (C<resolution>) or its address is in no answering document (C<address>); it returned nothing. A mask that no document answers is still an empty source unless it carries an address. An address is found the way a link finds its target, by the text of a heading as well as by C<:id>, and the first of two blocks sharing it answers; the last one used to. C<getDocIDs> is exported

=item a pattern C<item> finds first-level items, as C<item1> does; it found items of every level

=item C<*> finds any block a name would find, a paragraph written without a marker and a code block written by indentation among them. No pattern finds the blocks the tree adds around written ones: the root of a document, the root of a Markdown section and a folded section; the selection goes through them. C<outermost> keeps the found blocks that no other found block holds, in the order given

=item html output writes the C<:id> of a table of contents on its element, so a link to that address finds it

=item a selector source written without a scheme before the bar is read as a file, as in C<=include article.podlite | head1>; it was dropped, and the selection ran over every document given

=item a predicate C<[ :attr ]> holds for a value neither empty nor false by its kind: an empty hash no longer holds, while the number 0 and a list whose first element is empty do. C<[ :!attr ]> holds only for the value C<False>

=item the operation C<in> takes a selector as its operand: the terms of the definitions it finds, or a column of a C<=data> block, as in C<[ :status(in data:statuses#value) ]>. C<runSelector> takes C<SelectorOptions>, the current document and a reader of files, and throws C<SelectorError> when the source of such an operand does not resolve, has a format it cannot read, or lacks the address

=item a selector predicate reads a value in parentheses as a declaration does, as in C<para[ :author('John Doe') ]> and C<para[ :nested(3) ]>, and the operation C<in> with literal operands, as in C<[ :status(in 'draft','issued') ]>. An operation it does not define, C<in> without an operand, and an empty C<~<>> make the selector unreadable. Brackets, bars and whitespace inside a quoted value no longer split the selector

=item a selector C<para> finds a paragraph written without a marker, and C<code> a code block written by indentation. The text of an explicit C<=para>, of a heading and of a C<=defn> term is not taken for another paragraph

=item a selector reads the configuration the parser gave a block: a C<=config> no longer reaches blocks of another file, included or including, nor blocks after the block it is written in. A tree parsed without the plugin chain carries no such configuration, and a selector over it no longer applies its C<=config> directives

=item html output shows the caption of a table of contents, with the markup written in it and a hidden word hidden, and the wrapper of the table is written with C<class> rather than C<className>. C<tocTitleText> gives the caption of a tree that carries only the C<title> string, hidden as a whole when it holds a C<G> code

=item C<=table file:...> is reported with the code C<table-source-deferred>: the tree holds the address of the source and none of its rows

=item a table whose data source was read but holds no rows is reported with the code C<table-source-empty>, apart from a source that could not be had

=item a table of contents node takes an optional C<caption>, a caption block next to C<title>, and C<mkToc> takes it as its last argument; covered content is marked inside such a caption as well

=item C<parse> takes a C<recognition> list and fills it with the lines written as a directive of a block the parser does not know, and with the directive lines it could not read. The tree does not change

=item an C<:id> written without a value is not an address: a link to C<#true> no longer finds such a block

=item C<=test>, C<=fixture>, C<=assert> and C<=resource> are parsed as blocks. The last three keep their body as written, and the body of a fixture is not read as markup even under C<:allow>.

=item html and markdown output show a test open where it stands: its caption, the fixture and each resource as source, and what each assertion must or must not find

=item a heading with hidden text gets an address that is not made of its text, the same in production and draft, and it takes no place in the numbering of repeated headings

=item markdown output writes a named anchor, in lowercase, before a heading whose address it cannot build from the text: a hidden one, or one with an explicit C<:id>. Links to other headings follow the address a markdown reader builds, numbers of numbered headings included

=item a link that names a heading exactly is no longer taken by another heading whose shaped name matches

=item html and markdown output hide the caption of a hidden table, the title of a hidden boundary, the alternative text of a hidden picture, the title and file name written on a link in hidden content, and text in hidden content written with C<< S<> >>, an unresolved C<< A<> >> or C<< E<> >>, and the name a hidden block written in capitals shows as its heading

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item an explicit C<=code> block renders its C<:nested>, as in HTML and Markdown

=item a paragraph and code written without a marker render the C<:nested> and C<:masked> that C<=config> or C<=set> gave them, the paragraph of a definition too. An C<:id> given this way is written on the paragraph itself; an explicit C<=para> writes it on an element around the paragraph

=item an included C<.md> file is rendered as Markdown, and a file of another extension than Podlite or Markdown is read in the default mode; a C<:mime-type> on the C<=include> line decides how a file is read

=item an operand of C<in> in the selector of an C<=include> is read as C<convert> reads it: a file it names is asked of C<includeReader>, and an operand with no source reads the document the include is written in; both were looked for among the included files alone

=item C<includeReader> and C<expandPaths> may return C<undefined> for an answer that is not known yet: the include then brings nothing, its C<=set> waits with it, and no warning is given until every answer is known. A host that returned C<undefined> for a missing file gets no warning now; C<null> says the file is not there

=item includes are assembled by the assembly C<convert> uses, so a preview and C<convert> read a document alike: a C<=config> written before C<=include> reaches the blocks the include places, with C<:allow> and folding among its options; a table of contents of the including file reads those options. C<includeReader> and C<expandPaths> are called as before, with one change: a file included more than once is asked for once. A file of a mask may now be included by another file of the same mask, and a file that includes itself is passed over with no warning unless a C<=set> is lost with it. A C<=set> lost to an include that failed further in is named in the warning of that include; it was a warning of its own

=item on the first block an C<=include> brings, a C<=set> of the included file wins over a C<=set> written before the include for the same key; the one before the include won

=item a C<=config> adds its options to an earlier C<=config> for the same block or markup code, and decides the ones both set; it replaced the earlier one whole

=item with an include reader, the document is assembled before it is rendered: the blocks an include finds take its place, so a table of contents lists them, a link reaches them by their address, and a test groups with the included block above it. An included file takes its own includes first, and its own table of contents lists what they brought. A C<=set> before an include that brings no block reaches the next block; an include that fails, or stands in the way, loses the assignment and says so. A heading brought in under a masked block is hidden in the table of contents as in the text. A wrapper is told the files an included block came through in C<ctx.includeStack>, as before. A table of contents written in an included file still lists only that file

=item a C<=set> written before C<=include> reaches the first block the include brings, also when that block comes from an include inside the included file; the block after the include received it. When the include fails, the warning names the assignment it lost. When the include brings no block, the assignment is not carried on to the next block yet, and a warning says it has no target

=item an include by a selector renders a found block once: a block inside another found block comes with it and is not rendered again

=item a table of contents kept as a C<toc> or C<Toc> block renders its table, with the C<:id> of the block on the element

=item an include whose selector has an operand of C<in> that does not resolve brings nothing in and is reported as a warning, rather than rendering the include as failed

=item the caption of a table of contents renders the markup written in it, and a hidden word in it is hidden while the rest stays open

=item hidden content keeps its captions and titles hidden on the page: table captions, the summary of a folded block, the title of a note, the title of a table of contents, the alternative text of a picture, link titles and file names, text written with C<< S<> >> or C<< E<> >>, the kind of a hidden note, and the name a hidden block written in capitals shows as its heading. A link preview shows hidden text masked, a markdown block's included, except in draft

=item a C<=test> is shown where it stands: a line with its caption that opens the test in place, the fixture and each resource as source, and what each assertion must or must not find. The test is open in server output and folds in the browser unless the author wrote C<:!folded> or the address points at it; a block and the tests under it share a C<test-group> element for the site to lay out

=item a link preview leaves out comments and tests: their text no longer shows up in the preview of a section that holds them. A link to a comment finds nothing; a link to a test is shown the caption of the test

=head2 @podlite/toc

=item C<rebuildToc> builds the table a C<=toc> block holds again over a tree that gained blocks, such as those an include brought, reading the selector from the table and the configuration from the block; a table that keeps no selector is left as it is

=item the first non-blank line of C<=toc> is read as a selector: predicates and C<*> apply, and the lines after it are not read. Patterns are separated by commas. A selector that cannot be read, holds a markup code, names a source or needs a file makes a table without entries and a warning. The table does not list itself, and with C<*> it does not list comments, data or includes it does not name

=item a paragraph or code written without a marker is titled by its text, and a block whose name has no lowercase letter stands as a first-level heading; such a block used to stop the whole document from being built

=item C<=toc> and C<=Toc> stay blocks of the name they were written with, with their configuration and place, and hold the table of contents they make in their content. A selection finds them, C<*> among it, and a block already holding its table is not made again when the tree is built twice

=item the caption of a table of contents is parsed the way a picture caption is: its markup codes become nodes of the new C<caption> field, and a hidden word in it is marked as hidden. C<title> keeps the caption as written

=item an entry of a table of contents hides what its source hides: the text of a hidden heading, picture or table caption, a hidden word inside a heading, and every entry of a hidden table of contents. Open words stay open, and a link, note, alias or definition inside a heading comes into the entry as its text

=head1 0.0.90


=head2 podlite

=item the C<./lint> entry can be reached with C<import>. Its ESM build called C<require> in two places, and a module does not have that word, so the entry died on load. Reading a config written as code stays synchronous, so C<readConfig> and C<resolveConfig> keep their shape

=head2 @podlite/diagram

=item the package can be imported from Node directly. The emitted ESM referenced its own files without an extension, which the ESM loader refuses

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item the editor no longer goes through C<@uiw/react-codemirror>. It holds the layer between React and CodeMirror itself. Ordinary element attributes are still passed on, and C<basicSetup> is still accepted and still overridden, because the editor installs its own keymap over the standard one. Two things do change: a theme named as a string (C<light>, C<dark>, C<none>) is accepted but no longer selects a theme, and C<placeholder>, C<selection>, C<initialState>, C<onStatistics>, C<root> and the size bounds other than C<height> are accepted and do nothing

=item switching files while typing no longer leaves the cursor at the end of the new file. Incoming text now arrives before the saved position is restored against it, by construction rather than by timing

=item a file that saved no folded regions no longer inherits the folds of the one opened before it


=item declare the packages the editor imports but left to its neighbours to install: C<podlite>, C<@codemirror/commands>, C<@codemirror/state>, C<@lezer/common>, C<@lezer/markdown>, and C<tslib>, which the compiler puts in the emitted output. A consumer installing this package no longer depends on something else pulling them in


=item the body of an abbreviated block whose content is read as written — C<=table>, C<=for code> and the rest — is no longer handed to the markdown parser. A rule of equals between two rows of a table was read as the underline of a heading, and the row above it took the size and weight of one. Settings that go on over the next line keep their own reading and stay out of the body

=head2 @podlite/formula

=item the package can be imported from Node directly. The emitted ESM referenced its own files without an extension, which the ESM loader refuses

=head2 @podlite/highlight

=item the package can be imported from Node directly. The emitted ESM referenced its own files without an extension, which the ESM loader refuses

=head2 @podlite/image

=item the package can be imported from Node directly. The emitted ESM referenced its own files without an extension, which the ESM loader refuses

=head2 @podlite/markdown

=item the ESM build leaves declared dependencies to the consumer instead of inlining them. It used to resolve them by browser rules and bake in a character decoder that reaches for document, so importing the package from Node failed at load. The file went from 1.2 MB to 12 KB

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item the C<./node> entry can be reached with C<import> as well. Its ESM build called C<require>, which a module does not have, so loading it died on the first line


=item declare C<glob> as a runtime dependency, so that C<< require('@podlite/publisher/node') >> loads where the package is installed on its own. It was declared for development only, and worked until now because something else happened to install it.


=item the package needs Node 20.19 or newer. Its ESM build relies on the loader recognising module syntax in a .js file, which older versions do not do

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item the published build no longer carries a test file


=item the package needs Node 20.19 or newer. Its ESM build relies on the loader recognising module syntax in a .js file, which older versions do not do


=item the package can be imported from Node directly. The emitted ESM referenced its own files without an extension, which the ESM loader refuses


=item ajv moved from 7 to 8. Under the old version an ESM consumer got a namespace object where a constructor was expected, so the package could not be imported from Node at all

=head2 @podlite/toc

=item the package can be imported from Node directly. The emitted ESM referenced its own files without an extension, which the ESM loader refuses

=head1 0.0.89


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item shiki is a dependency again. Dropping it in 0.0.74 left every consumer without it, and their builds stopped resolving the module

=head2 @podlite/highlight

=item the README says what the runtime fallback covers: a chunk that fails to load after a successful build, not a missing install

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item the record contract, its types and the entry-or-page predicate, moved into its own module and is reachable as C<@podlite/publisher/record>

=item node-only code moved behind C<@podlite/publisher/node>. The package root no longer carries it, so importing the root from browser code no longer pulls in C<fs>

=item the plugin machinery got its own entry point, C<@podlite/publisher/plugins>

=item B<Breaking:> twelve names left the package root and now live behind C<@podlite/publisher/node>: C<convertFileLinksToUrl>, C<getDocumentAttributes>, C<getParserTypeforFile>, C<getPathToOpen>, C<getPublishAttributes>, C<makeLinksMap>, C<parseFile>, C<parseFiles>, C<parseSources>, C<processFile>, C<streamWriteArray>, C<streamWriteLines>. Code that took them from the root imports them from the node entry instead

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item shiki is a dependency again. Dropping it in 0.0.72 left every consumer without it, and their builds stopped resolving the module

=head1 0.0.88


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint reports a link whose local file is not there, and a link with no address at all. The file check is a warning, since a document under construction points at files that do not exist yet; the empty link is an error. A target with a scheme other than file, a bare word in a Podlite document, a home-relative path and input read from a pipe are all left alone

=item podlite lint adds the unclosed-markup-code rule, which the specification requires of a parser: a markup code terminated by the end of its block rather than by its own bracket is reported, so the line does not go out carrying the opening letter. Closure is counted the way the specification defines it, by matching bracket count or the closing guillemet

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item rendering the editor again with the same props no longer reconfigures CodeMirror

=item syntax highlighting comes from @podlite/highlight instead of a copy kept here, so the editor and its preview share one highlighter. shiki is no longer a direct dependency but a peer of @podlite/highlight: install it alongside. Type checking passes without it, and a chunk that fails to load leaves plain text rather than an error, but the name still has to resolve when you bundle

=item the editor can show the picture a directive names, under the line that names it, behind showInlineImages. An address the host cannot resolve leaves a quiet line rather than a broken icon, and the switch is apart from any switch over the raw text

=item the editor offers the names of settings in the header of a block, where until now it went quiet after the block name. Names already written in the document come first, then the ones the specification gives; the body of the document is left alone

=head2 @podlite/highlight

=item a caller that does not say how to wrap a code block gets the wrapping that shows a caption. The other wrapping drops it, and a caption written on a block was disappearing for callers that said nothing

=item the package carries the syntax highlighting that the renderer and the editor used to hold a copy of each, so both share one highlighter and one grammar cache. shiki is a peer declared optional: type checking passes without it and a chunk that fails to load leaves plain text, though the name still has to resolve when you bundle. A block with no lang attribute loads no grammar at all

=head2 @podlite/markdown

=item a markdown heading takes its own text as the id instead of a generated one, so a link written against the heading reaches it

=head2 @podlite/mcp

=item the package declares an exports map and its own type entry, and no longer points at an esm build it never produced. A bundler that preferred that entry resolved to a missing file

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item read the old field when a stored index predates the new one, so a site built from it keeps the behaviour it had

=item ask the document whether it is a page. A dated document is an entry unless it declares :type('page'). Nothing read that attribute before.
=item carry that answer on the record and in the page index, and export one predicate for it. The test used to be written by hand, against a field that says whether the address was declared.

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item a link is bound to its target on the tree, before the address is shaped for a format. A target that is not there no longer produces an address that merely looks like one: the link renders without an address, which is the form html gives a link that leads nowhere. Across the knowledge base this cut links pointing at nothing from 63 to 13

=item an identifier written with `:id` is shaped for the output the same way the address is, wherever the anchor is written. `:id<v1.2>` used to render the anchor `v1.2` against the address `#v12`, so a link to it never arrived

=item an image carries an alternative text only when the author wrote one. Every image exported to html used to carry the word `undefined` where the text goes; an alt that is absent is now left out, which html reads as «this image is part of the content», and one written as empty stays empty, which html reads as «decorative»

=item the address and the alternative text of an image are escaped in both exports, as a link already was. A quotation mark used to close the attribute, and a bracket used to end the address in markdown

=item an image `:alt` written without a value counts as not written. It used to put the word `true` where the description of the picture goes, and `:!alt` the word `false`

=item a link written without a bar keeps its address when the document is exported. Both the html and the markdown export used to put the node array itself where the address goes, so `L<#Setup>` came out pointing at `[object Object]`

=item a link whose target the author never wrote is exported as an anchor with no address, the form html gives that case, instead of one pointing at the word `undefined`. Markdown has no such form, so the text is written on its own

=item a quotation mark or an ampersand inside an attribute value is escaped in the html export. Without it a document could close the attribute and write markup of its own, and an address holding `&copy;` reached the browser as a copyright sign

=item an address holding a bracket or a space is written in angle brackets in the markdown export, the form markdown keeps for it. `man:find(1)` used to end the link at the first bracket

=item the formatting-code parser picks the closing delimiter while parsing instead of carrying a copy of two rules for every pair the language allows. The generated parser is less than half its former size and parsing is faster

=item a markup code that opens with a run of angles now has to close with a run of the same length. It used to fall back to a shorter one, so C<< C<<>> >> read as a code holding C<< <> >>; it reads as text now. All four delimiter pairs and the runs that carry a closing angle inside the content are unchanged

=item C<headingId> is reachable from the package entry, so any parser that builds a heading can apply the same rule the tree walker applies

=item the names of settings the specification gives are exported beside the block names, so the parser, the checker and the editor read one list

=item a section folded by :folded on its heading is grouped while the tree is built, so every reader of the tree sees it, not only the editor. The html export writes it as a native disclosure; markdown, which has none, writes the section out in full rather than losing the text

=item the document schema describes the tree the parser builds. An item carrying a checkbox, the terms of a definition, nineteen of the twenty-one markup codes, a semantic block holding more than one paragraph, and the entries of a table of contents were all outside it, and the check reported hundreds of errors on a sound document. The schema is generated from the types, so the types were brought to what the parser emits

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item a link pointing inside the document can carry a preview. The renderer takes a `linkPreview` handler, hands it the target and the text found there, and shows whatever the handler returns. Without a handler a link renders as before and nothing is looked up

=item the modules that held syntax highlighting are gone from this package: the shiki module and the code component moved to @podlite/highlight. Both were reachable as deep imports and now are not; the component is still exported from the package root

=item syntax highlighting comes from @podlite/highlight instead of a copy kept here. shiki is no longer a direct dependency but a peer of @podlite/highlight: install it alongside. Type checking passes without it, and a chunk that fails to load leaves plain text rather than an error, but the name still has to resolve when you bundle

=head1 0.0.87


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint adds the markdown-in-pod rule: a heading written with hashes, a row of bars and dashes, and a pair of asterisks arrive as text inside a pod block, and the message names the Podlite construct to write instead. Blocks that keep their content as written, the separator of a real table, and markup shown inside a code span are left alone

=item podlite lint adds the abbreviated-attrs rule: an abbreviated block takes the rest of its line as data, so attributes written there arrive as text. Reported only where the line holds nothing but attributes and content follows below; a named block, an example inside a code block, and an attribute written with nothing inside are left alone

=item the command no longer raises the diagram renderer, and with it mermaid and React, before it has read its arguments: only a conversion renders, so the plugin registry is loaded there. A check or a query now starts on a runtime that forbids loading an ESM package through require

=item podlite lint adds the dead-definitions rule: an =alias that no A<> calls, and a =data whose key no data: reference names, are reported. A mistyped key at the reference shows up as the definition going unused, which is the shape the mistake takes

=item podlite lint delimited-block-balance takes the markers inside a block whose name carries both cases as content, not as structure: such a block keeps its body as written, and an example written in it no longer reads as an unclosed block. An all-uppercase name is still read as structure

=item podlite lint table-column-width no longer reports a row whose cells are held apart by spaces while only the separator line draws bars: such a cell may fill its column to the last place and the table still parses. A cell standing on the boundary, and a cell pressed against a bar the row itself draws, are still reported

=item the check is reachable as podlite/lint — runLint for a whole run, lintFile and lintSource for the findings as data, with the types that describe them. The path no longer spells the build layout, and the check stays out of the package entry

=item podlite query --to json names the file every block came from, so a query over several files can be traced back to its documents. Text handed in on stdin is named <stdin>; the other output formats are unchanged

=item podlite lint checks a long list of files on several threads, which cuts a 126-file run from 1.64 to 1.14 seconds. Short lists stay on one thread, and the report reads the same either way

=item podlite lint finds .podlitelintrc.json or .podlitelintrc.js by itself — the nearest one at or above the first checked file, json before js. --config still wins, and --enable and --disable name a rule for one run

=item =include takes a recursive mask: file:./dir/**/*.podlite reaches the nested directories too, skipping hidden ones. A single-level mask stays within its own directory

=item =include takes a directory mask: file:./dir/*.podlite reads every matching file of that directory in name order, applies the selector to each, and leaves nothing behind when the mask names no file. Files the mask does not name are never read

=item podlite convert takes --render-mode with production or draft, and reads the same choice from PODLITE_RENDER_MODE when the flag is absent. Production stays the default and masks covered content; the flag wins over the environment

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item the HTML tag list no longer opens on every angle typed in a Podlite document, where the angle opens a markup code or an attribute value. A markdown file keeps the tag list, since raw HTML belongs there

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item a publish address written with a space in it is reported at build time instead of being shortened in silence: an unquoted value is read as a list and only its first word became the address. The message names the document and the address it got; quoting the value keeps it whole

=item the page index carries the record fields a site names in C<indexFields>, on top of the built-in ones

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item a selector reads the value it is given by the same grammar that read the declaration, and compares the value as a whole. An attribute holding a list is not equal to a string, whatever the list holds and in whatever order; membership, written with the tilde form, runs over the values as declared and no longer cuts a quoted string into words; a value of one kind is not equal to a value of another

=item a block whose name carries both cases holds its body as written, and the helpers say so: the specification hands such a body to a renderer or a plug-in, so a =begin written inside it is content

=item a table cell keeps the spaces inside it when the document is converted to markdown. Each fragment of the cell used to be trimmed on its own, which ate the space between a word and a markup code and around a sign the parser split the text on; only the edges of the cell are stripped now

=item covered content keeps its cover when it is taken out of the document it was written in. A block carrying :masked and a G<> code now mark the nodes below them, and the mark travels with the node through =include, through a selector and through a table built from a covered =data block. Draft mode still shows what production hides

=item name the header columns that were parsed when C«:columns» or C«:rename» cannot match one: leading spaces from an indented body stay in the field, so the name that fails looks identical to the one in the source

=head1 0.0.86


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint takes C«:lint-ignore» from the document: written on a block it silences the named rules there, written on C«=begin pod» it covers the file, and C«=config» spreads it over a scope. The attribute must name its rules; a mute that silenced nothing is reported under the lint-ignore rule, and the summary counts what was silenced

=item podlite lint applies the file given to --config: a rule set to off is not run, and any other setting replaces its severity. A config that cannot be read, or a setting outside off, error, warning and info, stops the run with exit code 2

=item podlite lint adds the table-column-width rule: a cell that reaches or crosses the column boundary set by the separator line is reported, because the table silently loses a column when it does

=item podlite lint reports a reshaped table under the table-shape rule, with file, line and a place in the summary; the padded-row message names the cause

=item podlite convert writes to stdout when the output is a dash, and reads the document from stdin after a dash or from a pipe with no files given; without a dash it still writes a file as before

=head2 @podlite/image

=item an image link belongs to its own block: an image with no link of its own is no longer wrapped in the link of an image above it

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item an image node carries its own link

=item fold a table cell onto one line when converting to markdown: a cell written as a block holds a paragraph, and its line breaks used to tear the row apart

=item attach a cell written outside a row to the row above it and report the recovery: an abbreviated C«=for row» holds only its own paragraph, so the cells under it used to leave the row empty and land at table level

=item C«:allow» written on a table reaches the text of its cells: an empty set keeps them literal, a named set leaves only those codes active, and a declaration on a row or a cell overrides the table

=item read a markup code configured by name, C«=config C<> :allow<I>» and C«=config V<> :allow<E>»: the declared codes act inside every following C«C<>» or C«V<>» whatever delimiters it is written in, and the declaration is held to the block it stands in

=item a table that was reshaped says so through the parse diagnostics instead of the console: a padded or dropped row, mixed separator styles and an unreadable data source now carry the place of the table

=item let a table cell hold markup codes in the document schema, so validating a table with C«C<>» or a link in a cell no longer reports errors

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item a code block wraps at spaces instead of breaking inside a word, so a long command stays readable
=item a table row is read from its first column: that column is set flush left, the columns after it stay centred

=head1 0.0.85


=head2 podlite

=item declare the node floor: the command line needs 20.19 or newer, because loading it reaches an ESM-only dependency through require

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item keep the page where it is when a change the editor did not make arrives while the editor is not focused: such a change reads as typing, and typing brings the caret into view by scrolling whatever contains the editor

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item resolve a component path that starts with C<../>, which was passed through unchanged and reached the build as written

=item read a component list written with spaces after the comma, which used to drop the whole import with a warning

=item import a component under another name

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item split a table row only on a separator that stands apart from the content: a bare pipe inside markup such as a link was counted as a column break

=head1 0.0.84


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint takes text from stdin, either after a dash or piped with no files given, and names it <stdin> in the report

=item podlite query and podlite lint hand over the whole output when it goes to a pipe; the command used to stop at 64 KB and still report success. A write that fails now gives a non-zero exit code

=item podlite lint delimited-block-balance takes =begin/=end lines inside a verbatim block as content and reports same-name nesting only there; nesting that the parser accepts is no longer an error

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item take the alias name from the text of the code, so C«A<NAME>» is replaced by its C«=alias» declaration in html and markdown instead of printing an object; a declaration is held to the block it is written in

=item read a markup code pre-configured with C«=config L<>» when rendering it, so a declared default reaches the link and a value written on the link itself wins

=item put pre-configured attributes on the blocks before markup codes are parsed, so C«=config code :allow<B>» and C«=config cell :allow<B>» decide which codes act inside those blocks

=item hold a C«=config» to the block it is written in, so a declaration inside one block no longer configures the blocks that follow it

=item keep a cell built from CSV or TSV as text, so a value reading C«Z<secret>» or C«C<command>» reaches the output whole; C«:allow» on the block names the codes that act inside such a cell

=item read configuration attributes on C«L<>» and C«W<>», so C«L<API|https://api.example.com :new :title('Doc')>» carries them into the AST instead of dumping them into the link specification

=item keep the closing angle of a markup code that ends with an attribute value in angles, C«L<Guide|https://example.fr/guide :lang<fr>>»

=item render the link attributes: C<:new>, C<:title>, C<:lang> and C<:download> reach html and jsx, C<:title> reaches markdown

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item render the link configuration attributes C<:new>, C<:title>, C<:lang> and C<:download>

=head1 0.0.83


=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item skip an image whose declared orientation is outside the range a build can read, instead of letting one such file stop the whole site

=head1 0.0.82


=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item C<:language> on the index page reaches site data, so a site states the language of its own content

=head1 0.0.81


=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item write the page records one per line, with a light index next to them that names every field but the parsed tree and says where each record sits in the file

=head1 0.0.80


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item apply the heading style to a markdown file as well; the previous release paired the tags only in podlite documents, so a heading in a C<.md> file kept body weight and colour

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item C<stateVersionPlugin> accepts optional C<indexFilePath>; C<globalStyles>, favicon and image files contribute to C<stateVersion>

=head1 0.0.79


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item a heading in a markdown file takes the same weight and colour as one in a podlite file; it carried only its size before

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item read a markup code delimited by a run of angles, C«C<<…>>» and C«C<<<…>>>», so a closing angle can sit inside the content

=item export a document holding the space-preserving code instead of stopping on it; the markdown and html exporters read its content as text now

=head1 0.0.78


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint link-target-resolves accepts a link to a heading by name — exact match first, then without regard to case; it used to know only author-written C<:id> and reported an error on a sound document

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item a heading in the editor is sized by its level again; the marker keeps its colour, the text takes the weight and size

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item shape the heading name into a url fragment when building an index anchor

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item a table row written on one line and separated by whitespace now splits on two spaces, as the specification says — the positional template it used to go through needed three, and a row with tighter columns collapsed into a single cell without a word
=item such a cell no longer carries the spaces that aligned it in the source
=item a heading keeps its own text as its identifier; shaping it for a url — collapsing separators, dropping punctuation, numbering repeats — moved to the output side, where each format applies its own rules
=item stop transliterating cyrillic headings: «Приветствие Мир» kept its letters instead of becoming Privetstvie-Mir, and the original name is recoverable again
=item headings that shape into the same fragment get numbered instead of sharing one anchor
=item markdown export shapes a same-document link the way a markdown reader will: lower case, punctuation dropped, each remaining space a hyphen, repeats numbered from C<-1>. Html keeps its own rule, where case survives
=item html export writes the shaped fragment as the heading anchor instead of the raw name — C<< <h2 id="Getting Started"> >> carried a space
=item html and markdown exports send a link to the heading it names: C<< L<text|#Getting Started> >> reaches C<#Getting-Started>, and a target written in another case still finds it
=item anchors are handed out in one walk before rendering, so a heading keeps the same anchor however often a renderer asks for it and the numbering no longer skips
=item add C<indexAnchors>, C<findAnchor> and C<resolveFragment>: a link target inside the document finds its heading by name, exact match first and then without regard to case

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item a link to a heading in the same document is shaped by the rules that shaped the anchor, so C<< L<text|#Приветствие Мир> >> reaches the heading; a target written in another case still finds it

=head1 0.0.77


=head2 @podlite/schema

=item a link keeps its target when the display text carries formatting codes — C<< L<I<text>|target> >> previously left the separator and target inside the text

=head1 0.0.76


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint attr-nested-angle also reports a value closed early by a C<< > >> written inside it, and no longer fires on running text or on the contents of a verbatim block

=item podlite lint attr-continuation-dropped catches a value written in any delimiter, not only angle brackets, and stays quiet inside blocks that keep their content verbatim

=item podlite lint adds the attr-value-dropped rule — reports an attribute whose value the parser could not read and dropped

=item podlite lint adds the link-target-resolves rule — an in-document anchor link must have a block carrying that id

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item give a section name and a name the author brought in colours of their own. A section name was coloured like a setting name, and an author's name like an unknown one, so four kinds of block name showed up as two

=item keep one copy of each CodeMirror package in the tree and raise C<@codemirror/view> to 6.43. With two copies an extension does nothing and says nothing: the find-and-replace panel refused to open, and highlighting stopped altogether when the copies were of C<@lezer/common>

=item read Podlite on top of the markdown parser: the content of a C<< =begin markdown >> block and code inside a fence get their own highlighting. This replaces the stream mode — C<src/podlite.js> and C<src/simple-mode.js> are gone, and with them the C<podliteLang> export

=item the tree highlighter tells a section name (C<SYNOPSIS>) from a name the author brought in (C<Image>) and from a standard block, in both the delimited and the abbreviated form

=item the body of a markdown block is a node of its own, so the editor tells markdown from Podlite by looking at the tree under the caret. C<getSuggestionContextForLine> no longer parses the document a second time; C<suggestionContextAt(state, pos)> answers the same question from editor state

=item a directive is read under an indent as well

=item read the content of an abbreviated block on its own marker line: C<< =item B<bold> >> and C<< =head1 C<code> >> now show markup codes there, and the brackets of a code are no longer taken for a setting value. Settings are read where settings belong — C<=begin>, C<=for>, C<=config>, C<=alias>, C<=set>, C<=boundary> and continuation lines

=item the fold service takes the blocks whose content is read as written from the highlighting tree instead of its own list of five names — folding is no longer offered inside a table, a formula, a picture or a data table

=item show content the author keeps out of what is published — C<< G<> >> — as a covered strip; the caret stepping inside uncovers it, and the text stays selectable underneath

=item colour the settings that follow the language in a markdown fence, C<< ```js :caption<...> >>, in Podlite documents and in C<.md> files alike

=item highlight all 22 markup codes the grammar accepts, not the ten the old highlighter knew: C<D>, C<E>, C<H>, C<J>, C<K>, C<N>, C<R>, C<S>, C<T>, C<V>, C<W>, C<X> are added, coloured by what each turns into when the document is rendered

=item in C<< L<text|target> >> and C<< W<text|context> >> the part after the bar is highlighted apart from the text; a bar inside a nested code belongs to that code

=item suggestions follow the caret: markdown snippets inside the body of a markdown block and in a C<.md> file, Podlite ones everywhere else. The markdown entries of the dictionary were unreachable before

=item the tree highlighter takes a hyphenated attribute name and a negation whole (C<:mime-type>, C<:!toc>), marks a value in a bare delimiter (C<'…'>, C<"…">, C<｢…｣>), and takes only a matching pair as a value

=item mark an attribute value the parser could not read, with the message on hover; the line under the caret stays quiet while it is being typed

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item B<Breaking:> square brackets and bare text in parentheses are no longer attribute values. C<:tags[a,b]> and C<:mime-type(text/csv)> now drop the attribute and report it; write C<< :tags<a b> >> and C<:mime-type('text/csv')>

=item report a directive line the parser could not read; each diagnostic now carries a code, C<value-unreadable> or C<directive-unreadable>

=item a value in brackets may continue on the next configuration line, the one starting with C<=> and a space; a quoted or angle-bracket value stays on its line, and a value left open is dropped with a message instead of taking the following lines with it

=item read the option form inside braces: C<< :key{:a<x>, :b} >> and C<:key{:!a}> next to the arrow form

=item a value that cannot be read no longer breaks the block: the attribute is dropped and the parser reports it on the root node as C<diagnostics>

=item read C<:key()> as an empty list and C<:key{}> as an empty hash instead of breaking the block

=item attribute value type is one of boolean, number, string, array or map; the untyped value tag is gone

=item attribute values in angle brackets hold a list when the content is separated by whitespace and a string otherwise; a comma inside angle brackets is part of the value, not a separator

=item attribute values accept the remaining delimiters from the configuration syntax table: bare single quotes, bare double quotes and corner brackets

=item add the C<< W<> >> contextual backlink markup code — same link shape as C<< L<> >>, rendered with a distinguishing class in HTML and JSX

=item html export emits C<:id> as an anchor on any block, not only on headings

=item markdown export keeps the checkbox state of task list items

=item a checkbox or numbering marker at the start of an item no longer drops the formatting codes written on the same line

=head1 0.0.75


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item stop importing global css from the entry — import C<@podlite/editor-react/Editor.css> and C<@podlite/editor-react/podlite-vars.css> in your app

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item stop importing global css from the entry — import C<@podlite/to-jsx/podlite.css> in your app (Next.js: in C<_app>)

=head1 0.0.74


=head2 @podlite/image

=item Image block keeps the src when its content is an inline image node

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item E<> renders string and text-node content instead of dropping it

=head1 0.0.73


=head2 @podlite/mcp

=item declare public npm access so the package publishes to the registry

=head1 0.0.72


=head2 @podlite/diagram

=item upgrade mermaid to 11 (async render API), fixing known security advisories

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item add a C<language> prop (C<podlite> | C<markdown>) that selects editor syntax highlighting; C<markdown> highlights Markdown source and fenced code by language

=item apply the Podlite fold service only under C<language> C<podlite>; Markdown folds by its own structure

=item add a top-positioned find-and-replace search panel

=head2 @podlite/image

=item render a picture caption as inline content — formatting codes and entities resolve, and a leading C<=directive> name stays literal instead of swallowing the caption

=head2 @podlite/mcp

=item initial release: MCP stdio server with four tools — C<podlite_parse> (source to AST with locations), C<podlite_validate> (parse errors + lint rules + schema check), C<podlite_render> (HTML/Markdown), C<podlite_query> (structural selector over blocks)

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item keep the C<!> negation marker on list attributes like C<< :!allow<G> >>

=item parse an empty attribute list like C<< :allow<> >> without breaking the block

=item keep the C<|> url separator in a C<< L<> >> link when the display text opens with guillemet quotes

=item export C<parseFormattingCodes> — parse a string into inline formatting-code nodes without block-directive parsing

=item resolve C<< E<> >> entities in the HTML exporter, matching the JSX renderer — named, decimal, and hexadecimal forms

=item resolve C<< E<> >> entities inside an inline C<> code span, so literal angle brackets can be written as C<< E<lt> >> / C<< E<gt> >> without ending the code

=item render a pipe table nested inside a structured C<=begin cell> as a grid instead of collapsing it to inline text

=item add the C<=set> directive: assigns attributes to the next block, with delimited and multiline alias value syntaxes, continuation lines, transparency through directives and C<=comment>, per-scope targeting, and a warning when no target follows

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item survive a crashing block renderer: the failed block degrades to an inline placeholder with a warning while the rest of the page renders; client-side, a per-block boundary keeps the preview alive on render errors

=head1 0.0.71


=head2 podlite

=item podlite CLI bin uses a node shebang so C<npx podlite> and C<pnpm exec podlite> run without Bun

=item podlite convert resolves C<=include> file references and applies the trailing C<| selector> filter

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item remove the C<:fallback> resolver, C<materializeFallback>, and its provenance metadata

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item remove render-through-C<:fallback> for unknown Mixed-case blocks

=head1 0.0.70


=head2 podlite

=item podlite lint C<syntax-valid> flags directive-like lines that fail to parse and are recovered as text

=item podlite convert accepts C<--base> (and env C<PODLITE_BASE>) to prefix relative C<file:> image paths

=item podlite convert renders plugin blocks (pictures, diagrams, formulas) through the full plugin pipeline

=item podlite lint C<media-absolute-file> flags absolute local image paths that a published site cannot resolve

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item add fallback chain resolver with cycle and depth detection

=item add C<materializeFallback> producing a derived view where a resolved block carries C<:original-name> and C<:resolved-via> provenance and the source tree stays unchanged

=item render unknown Mixed-case blocks through their C<:fallback> target type in the Markdown and HTML exporters

=item resolve relative C<file:> image sources against a C<base> option in the Markdown and HTML exporters; absolute paths and C<https:>/C<data:> sources pass through

=item omit an empty C<alt> value in the HTML image tag instead of rendering C<alt="undefined">

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item render unknown Mixed-case blocks through their C<:fallback> target type

=head1 0.0.69


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item entry now imports C<Editor.css> and C<podlite-vars.css> as a side effect. Consumers get styled output from C<import Editor2 from '@podlite/editor-react'> without a separate CSS import. C<sideEffects> manifest already lists C<**/*.css>, so tree-shaking is unaffected

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item C<=boundary> renders as a void C<<hr/>> without children. Previously the renderer passed an empty children array, which React 18+ rejects on void-element tags and crashes the preview pane

=item entry now imports C<podlite.css> as a side effect. Consumers get styled output from C<import Podlite from '@podlite/to-jsx'> without a separate CSS import. C<sideEffects> manifest already lists C<**/*.css>, so tree-shaking is unaffected

=head1 0.0.68


=head2 podlite

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=item replace top-level C<require('../package.json')> with a build-injected C<version> module. The published ESM no longer carries a C<require> call, so browser bundlers (Vite/Rollup) can lazy-load the package without C<require is not defined> at runtime

=head2 @podlite/diagram

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item add C<exports> map (with CSS subpath conditions), top-level C<types>, and C<sideEffects: ["**/*.css"]> to package manifest

=item add C<imageSrc> and C<imageBaseDir> props — forwarded to the default preview C<<Podlite>> renderer so image paths in C<=picture file:X> and markdown C<![](X)> resolve at preview time. Hosts that supply a custom C<makePreviewComponent> are responsible for forwarding these themselves. Type C<ImageSrcResolver> is re-exported for type-safe usage

=head2 @podlite/formula

=item add C<exports> map (with C<./default.css> subpath), top-level C<types>, and C<sideEffects: ["**/*.css"]> to package manifest

=head2 @podlite/image

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=item C<:image> renders through C<ctx.imageSrc> hook when the host provides one. Hosts pass the resolver via C<<Podlite imageSrc={fn}>>; C<=Image>/C<=picture> blocks and markdown images both flow through the same hook before C<<img>>/C<<video>>. Without the hook C<src> stays raw — preserves the previous behaviour

=head2 @podlite/markdown

=item lift C<types> to top-level package field (was only in C<publishConfig>), add C<exports> map and explicit C<sideEffects>. Consumers can now resolve types without an ambient declaration

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest
=item declare C<podlite> and C<@podlite/markdown> runtime dependencies (previously imported without being listed)
=item replace top-level C<require('../package.json')> in C<state-version-plugin> with a build-injected C<version> module. The published ESM no longer carries a C<require> call, so browser bundlers (Vite/Rollup) can lazy-load the package without C<require is not defined> at runtime

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=item C<toMarkdown> iterates into unknown Mixed-case named blocks instead of dropping them. The wrapper renders as a transparent container, its children pass through their own rules. C<exportHtml> behaviour unchanged

=item replace top-level C<require('../package.json')> with a build-injected C<version> module. The published ESM no longer carries a C<require> call, so browser bundlers (Vite/Rollup) can lazy-load the package without C<require is not defined> at runtime

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest. C<exports> conditions reordered with C<types> first

=item add C<imageSrc> prop on C<<Podlite>> for resolving image C<src> at render time. The hook receives a path and an optional base directory; it returns a resolved URL string or a Promise. C<:image> nodes (from C<=picture file:X>, C<=Image>, and markdown C<![](X)>) pass through the hook before C<<img>>/C<<video>>. Without the prop C<src> renders raw — preserves the previous behaviour. C<imageBaseDir> sets the base directory passed to the hook. Type C<ImageSrcResolver> and helper C<HookedImage> are exported

=head2 @podlite/toc

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=head1 0.0.67


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item C<HighlightedCode> now combines syntax highlight with inline formatting codes. When C<:allow<...>> permits fcodes inside C<=code>, B/I/C/U/K wrap their respective spans (C<<strong>>/C<<em>>/C<<code>>/C<<u>>/C<<kbd>> + C<fc-X> class) layered over shiki's syntax colors via the decorations API

=item shiki module loader uses a direct dynamic C<import('shiki')> so the bundler code-splits it into a separate chunk. Previously the loader was wrapped in C<new Function>, which is opaque to webpack and resulted in a runtime «Failed to resolve module specifier» in browser builds

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item C<=config item1>..C<=config item6> defaults now propagate to matching C<=item> blocks. The C<=config> propagation pass already handled C<=head1>..C<=head6>; the same C<item{level}> lookup is extended to items (plugin-items renames C<=item1> to a block with C<name=item, level=1>, mirroring the head case)

=item C<:numbered> inherited from C<=config> on items now promotes the wrapping list to ordered. When every item in a synthesized C<:list> has effective C<numbered: true> (either own or inherited), the list discriminator switches from C<itemized> to C<ordered>; an C<:!numbered> override on any item keeps it itemized

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item C<=code> blocks with C<:lang<...>> attribute now render syntax-highlighted via shiki. Inline formatting codes inside C<=code> (when C<:allow<...>> is set) layer on top of the highlight as wrapper elements (C<B<>> = C<<strong>>, C<I<>> = C<<em>>, C<C<>> = C<<code>>, C<U<>> = C<<u>>, others get C<span class="fc-X">), so bold and italic compose with syntax colors

=item shiki loaded via dynamic C<import('shiki')> so the bundler code-splits it into a separate chunk

=head1 0.0.66


=head2 @podlite/diagram

=item fix C<=Mermaid> crash on empty block content

=head1 0.0.65


=head2 podlite

=item C<< podlite lint >> accepts C<--strict>, C<--format text|json>, C<--config <path>>. C<runLint(files, options)> signature in C<@podlite/core>
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<syntax-valid> rule — catches parse exceptions, reports C<file:line:column: error: <msg> (syntax-valid)>. Text and JSON output. Missing files report C<io> error
=item C<< podlite lint >> exit codes: 0 clean, 1 violations found (or warnings under C<--strict>), 2 CLI/setup error (no files, unknown C<--format>)
=item C<< podlite lint >> file loader centralized — auto-detects C<.md> → markdown mode (via C<parseMd> from C<@podlite/markdown>), C<.podlite>/C<.pod6> → pod mode. Both produce the unified AST so rules apply uniformly
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<heading-hierarchy> rule (warning) — flags non-monotonic level jumps like C<head1> → C<head3>. Descending and equal levels allowed. Applies to both Markdown and Podlite headings via unified AST
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<id-unique> rule (error) — flags duplicate explicit C<:id<>> attributes in file scope. Auto-generated node ids ignored. Silent skip when the file declares no explicit ids
=item C<< podlite lint >> output split into C<src/lint/formatters/{text,json}.ts> modules. Text format emits a trailing summary C<N files checked, M errors, K warnings> and colours C<error>/C<warning> tokens with native ANSI when stdout is a TTY. JSON format unchanged (array of diagnostic objects)
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<attr-nested-angle> rule (error) — flags angle-delimited attributes whose value contains a nested C<E<lt>...E<gt>> (formatting code or raw angle) before the closing C<E<gt>>, which silently closes the attribute early under PEG tolerance. Suggests multi-angle / guillemet / parenthesis / bracket delimiters. Source-level scan via Pegmill C<grammar-lint.pegjs>; runs before AST parse so the rule is independent of parser tolerance
=item C<podlite().parse(text, opt)> dispatches to C<md2ast> from C<@podlite/markdown> when C<opt.mode === 'md'>. Default and C<opt.mode === 'pod'> use the pod grammar unchanged. Enables a single parse front-door for full-document Markdown input across consumers (C<@podlite/to-jsx>, C<@podlite/to-html>, C<@podlite/publisher>, C<@podlite/editor-react>)
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<delimited-block-balance> rule (error) — flags orphan C<=end>, mismatched C<=begin>/C<=end> pairs, C<=begin X> nested inside C<=begin X> (verbatim trap), and C<=begin> blocks left unclosed at end of file. Source-level scan via the same Pegmill grammar that hosts C<attr-nested-angle>; stack tracked through C<options._blockStack>, EOF check flushes remaining items
=item C<< podlite lint >> adds C<attr-continuation-dropped> rule (warning) — flags indented C<:attr<…>> lines following C<=begin X>/C<=for X> that get silently dropped by the parser (queryable only on the directive line itself). State machine clears on blank line, content line, or C<=end>. Suggests flattening attributes onto the directive line

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item highlighter recognises semantic and custom blocks in abbreviated form (C<=TITLE>, C<=NAME>, C<=SEE-ALSO>, C<=Diagram>). Previously only C<=begin>/C<=for> forms classified non-standard block names

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item extend C<parseOpt> with optional C<mode: 'pod' | 'md'> field. Consumers (e.g. C<@podlite/core>) can dispatch to a Markdown parser when C<mode === 'md'>. Pure type extension; schema's own parse unchanged

=item C<=config> block defaults are merged into matching blocks' C<node.config> during C<toAst>. Own attributes still override inherited ones. Renderers and pre-render passes that read C<node.config> directly (e.g. folded-section grouping in C<@podlite/to-jsx>) now see attributes inherited from C<=config>. For C<=head1>..C<=head6> the lookup matches by C<head{level}> (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=item C<:numbered> on a heading attaches an auto-numbered prefix to the head node as C<numberPrefix>. The pre-pass walks the document in source order, increments a per-level counter on each C<:numbered> head, and resets deeper levels on every head encountered. Renderers consume C<node.numberPrefix> and emit it before the heading text; C<exportHtml> wraps it in C<<span class="head-number">>, C<exportMarkdown> writes it inline. C<:!numbered> on a head overrides an inherited C<:numbered> default (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item C<<Podlite mode='md'>> now routes through the core front-door with C<{mode: 'md'}>, dispatching to C<md2ast> from C<@podlite/markdown>. Previously it set C<{podMode: 0}>, which ran the pod grammar in lenient mode and left top-level Markdown headings/blockquotes unparsed

=item C<:numbered> on a heading renders a number prefix inside the C<<hN>> element as C<<span class="head-number">N.M.K</span>>. Numbering counter is shared with C<exportHtml> via the C<numberPrefix> field attached during C<toAst>

=head1 0.0.64


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item fix syntax highlighting state leak from C<=alias> into following blocks (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=item editor highlighter distinguishes semantic uppercase blocks (C<SYNOPSIS>, C<AUTHOR>, C<SEE-ALSO>) and custom mixed-case blocks (C<Image>, C<Diagram>, C<Mermaid>) from standard blocks via new tokens C<semantic-block> and C<custom-block>

=item recognize C<=row>, C<=cell>, C<=data-table> blocks in highlighter; C<=data-table> body treated as verbatim like C<=data>

=item recognize C<=boundary> directive (single-line, accepts C<:caption>) and C<=set> directive (multiline continuation analogous to C<=alias>); state machines apply non-greedy keyword match to prevent state leak into subsequent directives

=item recognize C<G<>> guard fcode (dim/comment-style token, signals content concealed in production) and hash form C<{...}> in block attributes (e.g. C<:rename{a=>b}>)

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item C<stateVersionPlugin> accepts optional C<appVersion> parameter; appended to C<stateVersion> as C<+app<v>>

=item C<site-data-plugin> reads C<:theme> from root pod and emits C<@import '@Styles/themes/<name>.css'>; layered before C<:globalStyles> override

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item allow single-letter identifiers (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=head1 0.0.63


=head2 @podlite/schema

=item C<runSelector> predicates match attributes inherited from C<=config> defaults, resolved in source order

=head1 0.0.62


=head2 podlite

=item add C<< podlite query <selector> <files...> >> subcommand. Extracts blocks matching a selector. C<--to> selects C<podlite> (default, raw source), C<md>, C<html>, C<json>. C<--fail-on-empty> sets exit 1 on empty result. Match count on stderr unless C<--quiet>. Reads stdin when piped or when C<-> is given as a file argument
=item C<parseSelector> accepts bare pattern-list without a leading pipe (e.g. C<head1, code[:lang<python>]>); behaviour unchanged when the selector contains a C<|>

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item add C<=boundary> directive for marking structural section transitions. Optional C<:caption> attribute renders as C<title> on HTML C<<hr>>; Markdown outputs C<--->; JSX outputs C<hr> element
=item add C<=data-table> block for CSV/TSV rendering with C<:src>, C<:columns>, C<:rename>. Inline body and C<:src<data:KEY>> resolved at parse; C<file:>/C<http:>/C<https:> deferred to renderer
=item add predicate grammar to C<parseSelector>/C<runSelector>. Patterns are C<block-type[ :attr<value> ]> with wildcard C<*>, modifiers C<!>/C<?>/C<!?>, exact-match C<<v>>, list-contains C<~<v>>, AND via whitespace, OR via comma. C<ParsedSelector.blockFilters: string[]> becomes C<patterns: Pattern[]>
=item tighten C<parseSelector> scheme detection to identifier-shaped names so bare predicates like C<*[:attr<v>]> aren't consumed as a scheme
=item add C<G<>> guarded markup code. Production mode masks non-whitespace characters with U+2588; draft mode renders content visibly. Mode via C<toHtml({renderMode})> / C<toMarkdown({renderMode})>, default C<production>
=item add C<:masked> block attribute. Marks any block as guarded — content masked recursively in production mode, visible in draft. Idempotent with C<G<>>
=item add C<context> option to C<toAny>/C<toHtml>/C<toMarkdown> for passing initial render context to handlers
=item add C<maskText(s)> and C<collectText(content)> helpers
=item C<makeInterator>'s C<defaultFn> now returns the inner rule's result, enabling rule-chaining patterns (used by the masking interceptor)

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item render C<G<>> guarded markup code. Production mode replaces content with U+2588 chars wrapped in C<<span class="masked">>; draft mode shows content in C<<span class="masked-draft">>. Mode via C<<Podlite renderMode="draft">>, default C<production>
=item render C<:masked> block attribute. Block content masked recursively in production mode, visible in draft

=head1 0.0.61


=head2 podlite

=item add C<< podlite lint <files...> >> subcommand (stub; rules land in upcoming releases)

=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item fix C<=code> blocks with C<:allow<...>> losing fcode formatting

=item fix Next.js / webpack build error on shiki ESM import

=head2 @podlite/markdown

=item parse fenced code block attributes into structured config items (Specification.pod6 §2327-2407). C<```python :line-numbers :highlight<2,5-7> :caption<Example>> now produces an AST equivalent to C<=code :lang<python> :line-numbers :highlight<2,5-7> :caption<Example>>, replacing the previous raw C<:meta<string>> storage. Malformed attribute strings degrade gracefully — only C<:lang> survives (per spec §2380-2383). All seven configuration value types (Boolean, Integer, String, List, Hash) are accepted via the shared C<parseAttributes()> helper from C<@podlite/schema>

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item expose C<attributesOnly> as an additional PEG start rule for parsing a stand-alone configuration-info fragment (e.g. C< :line-numbers :caption<Example>>). Reuses the existing C<attributes> grammar rule — no shadow parser
=item add C<parseAttributes(src)> helper that returns an array of config items from a configuration-info string. Empty or malformed input returns an empty array (graceful degradation per Specification.pod6 §2380-2383). Primary consumer: C<@podlite/markdown> fenced-code-attribute parsing

=head1 0.0.60


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item fix task-list checkboxes invisible in full-preview mode — the C<content-visibility: auto> rule added in 0.0.44 was scoped to all C<.line-src> descendants, which re-introduced C<contain: size layout paint> on inner wrappers inside C<< <li> >> and broke the flex layout of C<.task-list-item>. Scoped the rule to direct children of C<.content> only; nested wrappers inside ul/ol/li/details/summary keep C<contain: none> from the existing carve-out, and the off-screen defer stays effective on top-level blocks

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item recognise the C<header> MIME parameter (RFC 4180 §3) on C<:mime-type> for CSV/TSV C<=data> blocks. When the C<=data> block declares C<:mime-type<'text/csv; header=present'>> (or the same with C<text/tab-separated-values>), C<=table data:X> marks the first row as C<:header>, which renders as C<<th>>/C<<thead>> in HTML and JSX. Default C<absent> preserves the previous behaviour. The MIME-type parser also accepts other parameters (e.g. C<charset>) without disturbing type matching. Spec: podlite-specs#25

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item C<:folded> on C<=head> now folds correctly when the heading is nested inside a named block, C<=begin nested>, C<=defn>, etc. — not only at the top level of C<=begin pod>. Previously the synthetic C<_folded_section> wrapper was created only for direct children of C<pod>, so folded headings inside other containers silently rendered as plain headings. The grouping pass now walks the AST recursively, so the spec's hierarchical-fold semantics (C<:folded> on a heading governs the heading and all associated lower-level content) apply uniformly
=item replaced C<margin: 1em 0> shorthand on C<details.folded> and C<details.folded-section> with long-hand C<margin-top>/C<margin-bottom>. The shorthand zeroed horizontal margins explicitly and (because of higher selector specificity) overrode host stylesheets that center block elements via C<details { margin-left: auto }>, pinning folded sections to the parent's left edge instead of aligning with surrounding headings. Long-hand vertical-only margins let horizontal margins flow through the cascade

=head1 0.0.59


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item fix Enter in C<=item> list and Tab/Shift-Tab level change now scroll the cursor into view — previously the caret could move past the viewport bottom without scrolling. Same fix applied to image paste/drop insertion
=item add C<includeReader> and C<includeBaseDir> props — forwarded to the default preview C<<Podlite>> renderer so C<=include file:X> resolves at preview time. Hosts that supply a custom C<makePreviewComponent> are responsible for forwarding these themselves. Without C<includeReader> the directive renders as nothing (preserves the prior behaviour). Type C<IncludeReader> is re-exported for type-safe usage
=item add C<expandPaths> prop — forwarded to the default preview C<<Podlite>> renderer for glob-pattern resolution in C<=include file:**/*.podlite>. Type C<ExpandPaths> is re-exported

=head2 @podlite/publisher

=item extend Selector parsing: C<file:> scheme with suffix-match against C<publishRecord.file>, and pipe-separated block-name filters (e.g. C<file:terms.podlite | defn, para>). C<include-resolve-plugin> now inlines every matched block at the C<=include> location, not just the first
=item add glob support in C<file:> selectors: C<**> (any depth), C<*> (within segment), C<?> (single char). Enables patterns like C<=include file:00-DayByDay/**/term-*.podlite | defn> for full auto-aggregation across a directory tree
=item move C<parseSelector> and C<runSelector> implementation to C<@podlite/schema>. These names are still re-exported from this package, so downstream consumers that import them from C<@podlite/publisher> keep working without changes. The move breaks a circular-dependency risk for renderer-side packages that need selector mechanics (e.g. live-preview C<=include> resolution)

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item resolve C<=table data:<key>> as CSV from a C<=data :mime-type<text/csv>> block (spec §1672) — table content is replaced with C<=row>/C<=cell> structure parsed per RFC 4180 (comma delimiter, C<"...">  quoted fields with C<""> escape). Spec §1672 does not define how to mark a header in CSV-sourced tables; authors who need a header row should use a structured table with C<=begin row :header> or a Markdown GFM table. C<file:<path>> scheme is left unresolved for host-driven readers
=item resolve C<=table data:<key>> as TSV when the C<=data> block carries C<:mime-type<text/tab-separated-values>> — split on tabs only, no quoting (C<"> is a literal character, unlike CSV)
=item add table error recovery — pad short rows with empty cells, truncate over-long rows; expected cell count is taken from a C<:header> row when present, otherwise from the row with the maximum cell count. Warn on each adjustment. Tables containing C<:colspan>/C<:rowspan> cells are skipped (naive count would mis-handle spanning)
=item warn on mixed column separator types within a single text-mode C<=table> (e.g. C<|> in one row, whitespace in another) — does not block parsing
=item improve CSV/TSV error recovery — missing C<=data> block leaves the table empty; a non-tabular mime-type is rendered as a C<=code> block so the underlying content remains visible
=item add per-line separator detection for text-mode C<=table> when lines disagree about separator type. A common pattern — header with C<|> for visual emphasis and data rows aligned by whitespace alone — used to collapse columns under the unified positional mask; each line is now parsed on its own and produces the expected cells. Tables with a single separator type, or with both visible kinds (C<|>+C<+>) handled by the shared mask, still go through the legacy positional path so continuation lines in multi-line rows keep their column alignment
=item move C<parseSelector> and C<runSelector> here from C<@podlite/publisher>. These are pure AST queries (no I/O, no filesystem) and belong in C<@podlite/schema>. C<runSelector> is now generic over a C<SelectorDoc> shape (C<{ file, node }>), so consumers without the publisher's richer C<publishRecord> type can use it directly. The publisher re-exports from C<@podlite/schema> for backward compatibility

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item fix C<wrapElement> no longer wraps C<=row>/C<=cell> nodes — those always render as C<<tr>>/C<<td>>, and HTML disallows arbitrary elements as children of C<<table>>/C<<tbody>>/C<<tr>>. Previously consumers that wrapped every node (e.g. an editor's line tracker injecting C<<div class="line-src">>) produced invalid HTML; browsers extracted the wrappers out of the table and collapsed cells into a vertical column. The outer C<=table> block is still wrappable
=item add C<includeReader> prop on C<<Podlite>> for resolving C<=include file:X> at render time. The reader receives a path and an optional base directory; it returns the included source as a string, or C<null> to leave the directive unresolved. The renderer parses the source, applies the selector (e.g. C<| defn>), and splices resolved blocks inline. C<ctx.includeStack> guards against include cycles. Without the prop the directive renders as nothing — preserves the previous behaviour for hosts without filesystem access
=item add C<expandPaths> prop on C<<Podlite>> for resolving glob patterns in C<=include file:X> (e.g. C<file:**/*.podlite>). The callback receives the pattern and an optional base directory; it returns a concrete list of paths. The renderer reads each path via C<includeReader>, builds a corpus, and applies the selector across all matching files. Without the prop, glob characters are read as literal path segments and resolve to nothing

=head1 0.0.58


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item add C<onSaveAsset> prop — paste/drop image handler. Clipboard paste (PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP) and drag-drop (same plus SVG) are routed through the callback, which returns a relative path; the editor inserts C<=picture path> at the cursor. No-op when prop is absent
=item fix performance: in full-preview mode skip C<view.dispatch({scrollIntoView:true})> and C<getBoundingClientRect> calls on the hidden CodeMirror view; coalesce preview scroll tracking to one C<getScrollMap> pass per frame via C<requestAnimationFrame>; apply the recorded line in a single dispatch on mode exit
=item fix UX: entering full-preview scrolls the preview to the editor's current top visible line (was always line 1); leaving full-preview places the cursor on the last visible preview line and centers it at ~1/4 from the top of the editor
=item add CSS containment (C<contain: layout paint style>) to C<.line-src> wrappers and C<.podlite-editor-preview> to reduce per-paint cost during scroll
=item add C<content-visibility: auto> + C<contain-intrinsic-size: auto 2em> on C<.line-src> in full-preview mode (gated by C<data-full-preview="true"> attribute) so off-screen blocks skip layout/paint until near viewport

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item added C<type: "map"> on config items parsed from C<:attr{k=>v, ...}> key-value syntax — previously the hash form returned the map value without a type annotation, making it indistinguishable from other value kinds
=item added C<makeAttrs().getMapValue(name)> helper — returns the first map-typed value as a plain object, or undefined when the attribute is absent or not a map
=item added guillemet bracket support («...») for formatting codes A, V, S, Z, D, X — previously only angle brackets (<...>) worked for these codes
=item refactored grammarfc to use parametric rules — unified simple codes via C<FCodeSimple> template, D/X via C<FCodeWithArray>
=item bumped Pegmill dependency from 0.1.0 to 0.1.2
=item added C<O<>> strikethrough formatting code (renders to HTML C<<del>>, Markdown C<~~...~~>)
=item replaced legacy C<Delete<>> alias with canonical C<O<>> — Markdown C<~~text~~> now produces AST C<{name:'O'}>. Helper renamed C<mkFomattingCodeDelete> → C<mkFomattingCodeO>
=item added C<H<>> superscript (renders to HTML C<<sup>>) and C<J<>> subscript (renders to HTML C<<sub>>) formatting codes
=item added structured table format with C<=begin row> and C<=cell> blocks, including C<:header> attribute support (renders to HTML C<<thead>/<th>>, JSX, GFM pipe tables)
=item implicit cell wrapping: non-cell children inside C<=row> are automatically wrapped in C<=cell>
=item unified table AST across text-mode, structured and Markdown inputs — all produce C<{name:'row'}>/C<{name:'cell'}>. Legacy C<table_row>/C<table_cell>/C<table_head> node names removed
=item added C<:colspan(N)> and C<:rowspan(N)> attributes for C<=cell> blocks. Values of 1 treated as default (no attribute emitted). Markdown export falls back to inline HTML C<<table>> when any cell has spans (GFM pipe-tables don't support colspan/rowspan)
=item tables with a C<:header> row now emit semantic C<<thead>>/C<<tbody>> wrapping in HTML and JSX. Tables without headers keep the flat C<<tr>> layout

=head2 @podlite/to-jsx

=item C<:folded> on C<=head> blocks now folds the whole section — heading plus every following node up to the next same-or-higher-level heading. Previously this attribute was ignored on headings
=item per-item conditional fold for C<:folded-levels> — only TOC items that have nested children become collapsible; leaf items render as plain list entries with no disclosure marker. Previously the entire level was wrapped as a single group
=item bare C<:folded> on C<=toc> wraps the entire TOC in a C<<details>>; summary shows the caption or "Contents" fallback
=item C<:folded-levels{k=>v}> key-value syntax is now rendered (value C<1> folds that level, C<0> keeps it open)
=item CSS: disclosure markers for folded headings and foldable TOC items sit in a left gutter so that text at the same level starts on the same x-coordinate regardless of whether the item has a marker. Heading and inline content stay on one line with the triangle

=head2 @podlite/toc

=item added C<:folded-levels{k=>v}> key-value syntax — each level can now be marked folded (C<1>) or not (C<0>) independently, e.g. C<{2=>1, 3=>0}>. Array syntax C<[2,3]> still works as before
=item added bare C<:folded> attribute on C<=toc> — wraps the entire TOC in a collapsible C<<details>>. Accepts C<:!folded>/C<:folded(0)> for a disclosure that opens by default

=head1 0.0.57


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item add C<enableFolding> prop — control fold gutter visibility (default C<true>, C<false> for embedded editors)
=item fix embedded editor preview: align first element with editor, reduce list item spacing
=item fix C<WindowWrapper> overflow — add C<max-width: 100%> and restore C<margin: 2rem> on C<.window>
=item fix fold gutter width compensation when folding disabled (C<padding-left: 11px>)

=head1 0.0.56


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item fix performance: eliminate synchronous re-renders on keystroke (text state → ref, deferred onChange via rAF, 300ms debounced preview)
=item fix performance: cache verbatim ranges in fold service (O(N) per call → O(1) via document identity cache)
=item fix search panel closing after Enter on first result — memoize extensions and basicSetup to prevent CodeMirror reconfiguration on re-render
=item add C<enableCopyPng> prop to C<WindowWrapper> — copies content as PNG to clipboard via C<dom-to-image-more>
=item refactor C<WindowWrapper>: remove dead SVG foreignObject screenshot code, add TypeScript types
=item fix fold markers appearing inside verbatim blocks (C<=code>, C<=comment>, C<=data>, C<=input>, C<=output>)
=item fix cursor not visible on file open — add C<autoFocus> + reset C<initialStateApplied> on file change

=head1 0.0.55


=head2 @podlite/editor-react

=item add list continuation on Enter: auto-insert C<=itemN> with type prefix preserved (C<[ ]>, C<[x]>→C<[ ]>, C<#>)
=item pressing Enter on empty C<=itemN> (or prefix-only) exits the list
=item add Tab/Shift-Tab to increase/decrease C<=item> nesting level (2-space indent per level)
=item add code folding for C<=begin>/C<=end> blocks and C<=head> sections with fold gutter
=item add C<initialEditorState> and C<onEditorStateChange> props for session state persistence (cursor, scroll, folds)

=head2 @podlite/schema

=item added C<foldedLevels> field in C<Toc> type and C<mkToc> helper

